If you get to "Fainting" then you've already made a couple mistakes. 
Nethack correctly solved the Rogue problem of "no food"; there are enough
sources of food (and nutrition!) available that this sort of thing would
end up being completely corner-case.

> then again, there are other items to consider that are consumed during
> severe starvation: clay, candles, tree bark, berries/fruits that are
> usually poisonous (soaked in water, then dried and ground into edible
> powder with little nutrition) dirt for minerals, etc.  I think this

Right.  If I let people eat their boots, I have to consider what else
people might be willing to eat, and where they get the big pot and water
to boil it in (given that potions of water are a precious resource), and
how they manage to boil it in a short time given that they're Fainting... 
etc.

It just doesn't add much to the game from the player standpoint.  New
players generally figure out Nethack's food economy/structure rapidly, 
and thus, don't starve, rendering this meaningless -- after all, leather
certainly couldn't have a high nutritional value for a @/h/o/G.  In the
end, it's more productive to just eat corpses (as they're doing already).

